Template: krauken/replace-brewpi
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Disable the existing BrewPi Remix service?
 An active brewpi.service was detected on this system. Krauken fully
 replaces BrewPi Remix's control daemon and cannot share the same serial
 port with it -- both processes fighting over the same Arduino will
 corrupt both (and krauken-daemon's own systemd unit already declares a
 hard Conflicts= against brewpi.service, so at most one can run anyway).
 .
 If you accept, brewpi.service will be stopped and disabled now -- not
 uninstalled. Your existing BrewPi Remix installation is left in place at
 /home/brewpi, and you can re-enable it by hand at any time with
 `systemctl enable --now brewpi` if you ever need to revert.

Template: krauken/has-krauken-hardware
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Are you installing this on genuine Krauken hardware?
 Krauken's own custom PCB carries an ID EEPROM that lets this software
 auto-discover the hardware, the same way a Raspberry Pi HAT normally
 works. Building the small tool that provisions that EEPROM
 (krauken-hwid) needs a C compiler and a few minutes on first install;
 skip it entirely if you're only using a BrewPi-compatible Arduino
 and/or a Tilt hydrometer, since neither needs it.
 .
 This question is currently dormant -- it isn't asked yet because the
 Krauken PCB itself hasn't been released, so nobody could truthfully
 answer yes. The default (skip) is always what applies today.
